  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Timur breaks up the quarrel between Senya and Fedya by proposing democratic kitchen elections: each candidate will run the kitchen for half the day, and then the cooks will vote. Meanwhile, a card debtor arrives outside to pay Mikhail Jekovich with a horse.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тихо, не надо сейчас спорить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Устроим здесь честные выборы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пусть каждый побудет немного главным.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Народ посмотрит и потом выберет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Значит, я буду первый главный.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "А я буду второй главный.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Всё, регистрация кандидатов уже окончена.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вечером мы проведём наши выборы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "А если будут большие пробки?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ну, сегодня повезло тебе, Миша.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Михаил Джекович коня в карты выиграл.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Конь приехал старый долг отдать?",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тихо, не надо сейчас спорить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Quiet, there is no need to argue now.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'quiet' and finishing on 'to argue'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Устроим здесь честные выборы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let us hold fair elections here.' In Russian, the idea of 'arrange' flows naturally into 'elections' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пусть каждый побудет немного главным.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let each of you be the boss for a bit.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'let' and finishing on 'important'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Народ посмотрит и потом выберет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The people will watch and then choose.' Notice how Russian expresses 'the people' and 'will choose' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Senya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Значит, я буду первый главный.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'So I will be the first boss.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'so' before landing on 'main'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "А я буду второй главный.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And I will be the second boss.' Fedya brings out the contrast between 'and' and 'I' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Всё, регистрация кандидатов уже окончена.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That is it, candidate registration is already closed.' Timur anchors the line on 'everything', while 'finished' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вечером мы проведём наши выборы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In the evening we will hold our elections.' The grammar packs 'in the evening' and 'elections' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "А если будут большие пробки?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what if there is heavy traffic?' In Russian, the idea of 'and' flows naturally into 'traffic jam' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ну, сегодня повезло тебе, Миша.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, you got lucky today, Misha.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'come on' and finishing on 'Misha'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Михаил Джекович коня в карты выиграл.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Mikhail Jekovich won a horse at cards.' Fedya anchors the line on 'Mikhail', while 'won' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Конь приехал старый долг отдать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did the horse arrive to pay off an old debt?' Senya brings out the contrast between 'knight' and 'came' in a few short words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
